ion/answer-fields "answer-field-bdfbe9cc-d10f-546a-ae0e-e1474b7f55ea"] [17592186115330 :knowledge-point/questions "question-586758ad-b0a5-531e-b46a-bdc8f3f5617e"] ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/id #uuid "54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4"] ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93048"] ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/problem "Find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2.$"] ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $q(x)=2\\sqrt[4]{x+3}-2,$ we replace $x$ with $x+3$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\nx+3\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=q(x)$ is $x\\in [-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/97/977b12dd8bd88d2b79285ef07d89f9958194fc84f340d7930c5b40122f8896d2.png)"] ["question-54e659be-f31e-5f00-888e-77687d0dffe4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-229676b2-2ee0-5238-ae2a-c792f196f8eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "229676b2-2ee0-5238-ae2a-c792f196f8eb"] ["answer-c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" :answer/id #uuid "c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28"] ["answer-c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c4a84016-a105-56d3-be93-bb62a2c31e28" :answer/value "x\\in (\\infty ,-3]"] ["answer-2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" :answer/id #uuid "2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752"] ["answer-2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2a96d10d-da6d-5545-aa04-dba500589752" :answer/value "x\\in (\\infty ,3]"] ["answer-29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc661" :answer/id #uuid "29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc661"] ["answer-29de12d5-e988-519a-b0a4-78f0306fc66